    "q-143628": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143628",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Mike has a total of $100$ candies divided into three bags. The first bag contains $18$ mint-flavored candies, while the second bag contains $16$ mint-flavored candies. If $55%$ of all of Mike's candies are mint-flavored, what is the probability that a randomly selected candy is a mint-flavored candy from the third bag?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $M$ be the event that the selected candy is mint-flavored, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected candy is from the first bag,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected candy is from the second bag,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected candy is from the third bag.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap A)+P(M\\cap B)+P(M\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(M\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(M\\cap A),$ we take the number of mint-flavored candies in the first bag $(18)$ and divide by the total number of candies $(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap A)=\\frac{18}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(M\\cap B),$ we take the number of mint-flavored candies in the second bag $(16)$ and divide by the total number of candies$(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap B)=\\frac{16}{100}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(M)=55%=\\frac{55}{100}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(M\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap A)+P(M\\cap B)+P(M\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{55}{100} & =\\frac{18}{100}+\\frac{16}{100}+P(M\\cap C) \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =\\frac{55}{100}-\\frac{18}{100}-\\frac{16}{100} \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =\\frac{21}{100} \\\\ P(M\\cap C) & =21%\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
