    "q-143722": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143722",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A total of $200$ colored table tennis balls are distributed among three boxes, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $20$ green balls, while Box $B$ contains $30$ green balls. If $45%$ of all the balls are green, what is the probability that a randomly selected ball is a green ball from Box $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $G$ be the event that the selected ball is green, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(G\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap A),$ we take the number of green balls in box $A$ $(20)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap A)=\\frac{20}{200}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap B),$ we take the number of green balls in box $B$ $(30)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap B)=\\frac{30}{200}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(G)=45%=\\frac{90}{200}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{90}{200} & =\\frac{20}{200}+\\frac{30}{200}+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{90}{200}-\\frac{20}{200}-\\frac{30}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =20%\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
