    "q-143747": {
      "dom_id": "step-q143747",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Three bags contain marbles. Bag $1$ contains $12$ red marbles and $8$ green marbles. Bag $2$ contains $14$ red marbles and $6$ green marbles. And bag $3$ contains $10$ red marbles and $10$ green marbles. A bag is selected at random, and a marble is randomly selected from the bag. What is the probability that the marble is red?",
      "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be the event of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the marble is from bag $1,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the marble is from bag $2,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the marble is from bag $3.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a bag is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $12$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $1,$ $14$ red marbles out of $20$ in bag $2,$ and $10$ red marbles out of $20$  in bag $3,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{12}{20},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{14}{20},\\,P(R|C)=\\frac{10}{20}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a red marble at random from one of the three bags is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{14}{20})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{10}{20})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{60}+\\frac{14}{60}+\\frac{10}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{60} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
